~ start
NIÑA: Ate Pili! 
NIÑA: Valerie! Ollie and Kiki too! It has been a while since you visited our store. Are you buying supplies for the weekend too?

PILI: Niña! Look at you! Your bump is getting so big now. How many months is it already?

NIÑA: Entering our seventh month next week, ate. Ken is already panicking. He spends every night talking to my belly telling the baby to wait until the workshop is fully repaired.

KEN: Magandang araw po, Ate Pili. I'm just making sure our roof doesn't leak, love. With the typhoon report on the television yesterday, I wanted to buy these batteries early so Niña isn't left in the dark if the power lines go down tomorrow.

PILI: You are a good husband, Ken. Hardworking, just like your father. Don't worry too much, anak. God always provides for young families who work hard. Look at us! Tomorrow is our Ollie’s eighth birthday! We came all the way here just to get the supplies for his birthday.

NIÑA: Oh, Advanced Happy Birthday, Ollie! Turning eight already? Wow, you're a big boy now. 
NIÑA: Next time you pass by our store, remind me to give you your favorite sweet crackers, okay?

PILI: Thank you, anak. Since you two live just down the lane, why don't you drop by our house tomorrow afternoon? It's going to be a birthday party just for our Ollie. 
PILI: Please come! There's always enough food for our favorite neighbors.

NIÑA: Ate Pili... we would love to come! I'll make sure Ken finishes his workshop repairs early tomorrow so we can come.

KEN: Happy Birthday, Ollie. 
KEN: ...
KEN: Ate Pili... is Mang Ernesto back at the farm? The wind coming from the coast this morning felt a bit heavy. I can drop by your house later with my tools if he needs help securing the side window shutters.

VALERIE: Thank you, Kuya Ken but Tatay and Dodong as well as Noni already went out early to tie down the tarpaulins over the copra beds. Tatay says our coconut trees will block the worst of the wind anyway.

KEN: Even Noni too? He’s a brave kid.
KEN: And that’s true. The old trees in Taklub have deep roots. As long as the farm equipment stays dry, we'll all pull through just fine. I just... I want to make sure this store is stocked up so Niña doesn't have to strain herself this month.

NIÑA: You are doing more than enough, love. Don't carry the whole world on your shoulders today. Look, we have our batteries, we have our rice and we have each other. Everything is going to be beautiful when our little one gets here.

KIKI: Baby! See baby!

NIÑA: Yes, Kiki! There's a little baby inside here. Next year, you can play together in the yard, okay?

PILI: Thank God... seeing you two so devoted to each other fills my heart. You know, Niña, when I was pregnant with our Valerie, Ernesto was exactly like Ken. He wouldn't let me lift a single basin of water and he spent every night whispering to my belly. He always promises he'd build a safe house for us.
PILI: It's scary at first and the worries never really stop. But when you look down at your children growing up right in front of you... every single sleepless night and every ounce of hard labor becomes worth it. You two are going to be wonderful parents. I just know it.

NIÑA: Thank you, Ate Pili. Your words mean everything to us.

PILI: Geez. Look at me getting all sentimental in the middle of a grocery store. We should get going now. See you both tomorrow.

VALERIE: Say goodbye to Kuya Ken and Ate Niña, Ollie. Help Nanay push the cart forward.

OLLIE: Thank you, Kuya Ken and Ate Nina! Ingat po!

=> END